"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Preoperative PSA was 10. The surgical findings included a Gleason 3+2 with tertiary pattern 4 (ISUP grade group 1), pT3a stage with no extraprostatic extension, positive surgical margins, and seminal vesicles not invaded. Lymphovascular invasion was present, and no lymph nodes were removed. The biopsy showed a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2). The absence of documented nodal sampling widens the uncertainty regarding regional disease control.\n\nCountervailing findings include the positive surgical margins and the presence of lymphovascular invasion. The difference between the biopsy grade (3+4) and the prostatectomy grade (3+2) is noted. The postoperative PSA and its subsequent trajectory are critical missing pieces of information that would significantly revise this prognosis. The current uncertainty regarding nodal status due to missing sampling is a key limitation. The fixed estimated horizon is 69.39 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 41.64–97.15 months; its coverage has not been validated."
